{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 80,
    "dDurationMs": 8130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In our search for alien lifeforms we scan\nfor primitive biosignatures, and wait and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 8210,
    "dDurationMs": 3589,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hope for their errant artificial signals to happen by the Earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 11799,
    "dDurationMs": 2661,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But these may not be the best way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14460,
    "dDurationMs": 6090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Any energy-hungry civilization more advanced\nthan our own may leave an indisputable technological"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20550,
    "dDurationMs": 1700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mark on the galaxy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 22250,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And yes, we’re very actively searching for those too."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25530,
    "dDurationMs": 4176,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Time to update you on the hunt for galactic\nempires."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34925,
    "dDurationMs": 4575,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Humanity is an energy-hungry species, and\nthat hunger grows exponentially."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 39500,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fortunately we have a near-inexhaustible power source raining down from above."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44100,
    "dDurationMs": 4660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We could satisfy humanity’s current energy\nneeds by covering a tiny fraction of Earth’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 48760,
    "dDurationMs": 4583,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "surface with solar cells. And we should probably do that by the way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53343,
    "dDurationMs": 3067,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But we can expand that area indefinitely."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 56410,
    "dDurationMs": 4510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Not by covering the entire Earth, but by building solar collectors in space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60920,
    "dDurationMs": 7880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s a lot of room to expand if we were to cover the entire sphere surrounding the Sun with the radius of Earth’s orbit,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68800,
    "dDurationMs": 4660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we’d collect all of the Sun’s light - a\nbillion times more than what we’d get just"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73460,
    "dDurationMs": 2610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "covering the planet in solar cells."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 76070,
    "dDurationMs": 3940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The idea of wrapping the sun in solar collectors is not new."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80010,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It was first proposed by British author Olaf\nStapledon in his 1937 story Star Maker."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 87450,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It was also named the Stapledon sphere - but only by the guy who was trying to avoid having"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 93770,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "his own name attached to the idea, without\nsuccess."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98030,
    "dDurationMs": 6730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In 1960, Freeman Dyson was inspired by Stapleton’s fiction to propose that a highly advanced"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 104760,
    "dDurationMs": 6380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "civilization might expand to occupy an entire\nsphere surrounding its home star at the radius"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 111140,
    "dDurationMs": 5250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of its planet of origin, for the purpose of both habitation and energy collection."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116390,
    "dDurationMs": 5269,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To Dyson’s regret, the notion became called the Dyson sphere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 121659,
    "dDurationMs": 3971,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Naturally we’ve talked about the plausibility\nof Dyson spheres in the past - long story"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 125630,
    "dDurationMs": 6700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "short, not plausible as a solid shell, but\npretty reasonable actually as a swarm of independent"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 132330,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "craft, either in orbit around the star or\nkiting in place on the solar wind."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 137410,
    "dDurationMs": 4270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But Freeman Dyson’s original paper was not\nabout how to build one of these things -"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 141680,
    "dDurationMs": 2279,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rather how to find them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143959,
    "dDurationMs": 5181,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If the Dyson sphere - or swarm or whatever\n- is a natural stage in the progression of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 149140,
    "dDurationMs": 5420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "many civilizations, might these be the best\nway to look for signs of intelligent life?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154560,
    "dDurationMs": 3560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s find out what our search has told\nus."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 158120,
    "dDurationMs": 4850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earth’s biosphere is already a pretty good\nplanet-scale solar collector."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 162970,
    "dDurationMs": 4460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you were to observe Earth from a distant\nsolar system, you might notice that it looks"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 167430,
    "dDurationMs": 1980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "strangely dark."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 169410,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It doesn’t reflect nearly as much of the\nSun’s light as you’d expect for a globe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 173790,
    "dDurationMs": 4930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of water and rock - it's as though that energy is being sucked up."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 178720,
    "dDurationMs": 5390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if you shifted your gaze from visible\nlight to infrared, you’d find all of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 184110,
    "dDurationMs": 1140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "missing energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 185250,
    "dDurationMs": 8560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "See, the Sun produces a thermal spectrum - light generated by its 6000K surface is distributed at all wavelengths,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 193810,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but it peaks in the visible part of the spectrum."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197490,
    "dDurationMs": 4170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And Earth’s biosphere - mostly its forests\n- absorb a fraction of that light and use"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 201660,
    "dDurationMs": 3260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it to power biological processes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 204920,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And in the process they warm up, shedding\nall of that energy as a new thermal spectrum,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 210860,
    "dDurationMs": 5290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "now at 300 or so Kelvin, with its peak at\ninfrared wavelengths."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 216150,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To spot this spectral shift from another star\nyou’d have to have an incredibly sensitive"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 221310,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "telescope to pick the Earth’s faint glow\nfrom the Sun’s overwhelming brightness."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 227370,
    "dDurationMs": 5670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if a civilization has expanded to reprocess\na significant fraction of its home star’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 233040,
    "dDurationMs": 5370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light, then not only could we detect that\nshift with our current telescopes, it would"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 238410,
    "dDurationMs": 2250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be difficult to miss."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 240660,
    "dDurationMs": 5680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that was also true even back in 1960 when Dyson proposed that we look for these things."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 246340,
    "dDurationMs": 4649,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So how do you go about searching for a Dyson sphere?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 250989,
    "dDurationMs": 5550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Dyson’s original notion was simply to search\nfor points of light with temperatures of a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256539,
    "dDurationMs": 4540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "few hundred Kelvin, but emitting the power\nof an entire star."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 261079,
    "dDurationMs": 5110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Anything capable of producing that much power at that low a temperature must be huge - the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 266189,
    "dDurationMs": 2870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "size of a planetary orbit."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 269059,
    "dDurationMs": 7670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In a 1966 paper, Carl Sagan and Russell Walker added some details to the calculations, figuring"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 276729,
    "dDurationMs": 5717,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out how close a Dyson sphere would need to\nbe in order to be detected with the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 282446,
    "dDurationMs": 1904,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "technology of the time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 284350,
    "dDurationMs": 3089,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Short story, it's would have to have been pretty close."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 287439,
    "dDurationMs": 7207,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But our infrared telescopes advanced quickly, and crucially they found their way into space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 294646,
    "dDurationMs": 3964,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Surveys started to find many, many objects\nthat fit the description."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 298610,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Unfortunately, there are some obvious natural\nexplanations for a planetary-orbit-sized object"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 304970,
    "dDurationMs": 1819,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at a few hundred Kelvin temperature."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 306789,
    "dDurationMs": 4141,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example we have protostars - the clouds\nof gas in the process of collapsing into a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 310930,
    "dDurationMs": 6790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "new star, circumstellar disks - the luke-warm\ndisk of gas surrounding a new-born star that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 317720,
    "dDurationMs": 2890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will eventually form planetary systems."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 320610,
    "dDurationMs": 6019,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It turns out it’s near impossible to identify\na full Dyson sphere from its thermal emission"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 326629,
    "dDurationMs": 1160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "alone."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 327789,
    "dDurationMs": 5301,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sagan and Walker suggested that we consider these infrared sources as candidate Dyson"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 333090,
    "dDurationMs": 5799,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spheres, to be followed up by scanning for\nnarrow-band radio emission, a much more telling"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 338889,
    "dDurationMs": 1721,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "signature of technology."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 340610,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So yeah, we could go flicking through the\nfrequency channels of gas clouds looking for"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 344770,
    "dDurationMs": 1100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "alien TV shows."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 345870,
    "dDurationMs": 2919,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or we could think about this a bit more."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 348789,
    "dDurationMs": 6150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Dyson and Sagan etc’s calculations were\nfor a full Dyson sphere - one that reprocesses"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 354939,
    "dDurationMs": 1720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all of their star’s light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 356659,
    "dDurationMs": 5634,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But many civilizations may find it totally\nadequate to only partially harvest their star’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 362293,
    "dDurationMs": 871,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 363164,
    "dDurationMs": 5375,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Partial Dyson spheres could take the form\nof equatorial rings of collectors, or swarms"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 368539,
    "dDurationMs": 2660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that don’t fill the entire spherical surface."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 371199,
    "dDurationMs": 6131,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or really any so-called megastructure that\nintercepts a decent fraction of the star’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 377330,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 378330,
    "dDurationMs": 5859,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It turns out that a partial Dyson sphere might\nbe even more detectable than a full one."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 384189,
    "dDurationMs": 5870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To understand how to spot such a thing, let’s\nlearn a thing or two about stars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 390059,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Stars are surprisingly simple beasts, for\nthe most part ruled by laws of physics that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 395039,
    "dDurationMs": 2141,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we’ve understood for centuries."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 397180,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A star in the prime of its life settles into\nan equilibrium state in which the outward"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 402490,
    "dDurationMs": 5359,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "flow of fusion-generated energy balances the\ninward crush of its own gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 407849,
    "dDurationMs": 4891,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you know the mass of such a star, you can\npredict its size, its temperature, its brightness,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 412740,
    "dDurationMs": 1840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its lifespan, and so on."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 414580,
    "dDurationMs": 5869,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If a star deviates from the tight relationships\nbetween these properties then we quickly know"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 420449,
    "dDurationMs": 2120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that something is up."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 422569,
    "dDurationMs": 5151,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, it might be surrounded by a Dyson sphere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 427720,
    "dDurationMs": 3779,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s think a bit about what that will look like."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 431499,
    "dDurationMs": 5370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It would be an unnatural combination of a\nlarge, hot object - the central star, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 436869,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a gigantic cool object - the partial sphere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 440309,
    "dDurationMs": 4100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Each would produce its own thermal spectrum at different temperatures."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 444409,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But from our great distance the light from\nthe star and the sphere would be blended into"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 449349,
    "dDurationMs": 1711,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a point."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 451060,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Two pure thermal spectra would be stitched\ninto one weird spectrum with too little light"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 457240,
    "dDurationMs": 4739,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at visible wavelengths and too much at infrared wavelengths."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 461979,
    "dDurationMs": 5610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we carefully broke up the star’s light\nwith spectrographs spanning a huge wavelength"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 467589,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "range, we might be able to see two distinct\nthermal spectra."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 472749,
    "dDurationMs": 5151,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that’s a lot of work even for a single\nstar, and we probably need to search a huge"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 477900,
    "dDurationMs": 5199,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "number of stars to hope to find even a single\nDyson sphere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 483099,
    "dDurationMs": 6850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fortunately, even a crude observation of a star’s color and brightness can tell us"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 489949,
    "dDurationMs": 2220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that something is up."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 492169,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In astronomy, color refers to the ratio of brightnesses at two different wavelengths."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 497629,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For something with a pure thermal spectrum,\nwe can exactly identify its temperature from,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 503929,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "say, the ratio of red light to blue light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 507289,
    "dDurationMs": 4340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I mentioned that most stars show a tight relationship\nbetween certain properties."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 511629,
    "dDurationMs": 4671,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, the higher the surface temperature of a star, the higher its total power output"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 516300,
    "dDurationMs": 2050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- it’s luminosity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 518350,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we graph temperature against luminosity\nwe get something called a Hertzsprung-Russell"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 523070,
    "dDurationMs": 1510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "diagram."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 524580,
    "dDurationMs": 6010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For temperature we can just use color - because that’s easier to measure."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 530590,
    "dDurationMs": 4300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "All stars that are in the primes of their\nlives - those powered by fusing hydrogen into"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 534890,
    "dDurationMs": 8410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "helium in their cores - lie on this tight\nband called the main sequence."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 543300,
    "dDurationMs": 4970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Stars drive off the main sequence when they\nexpand into giants at the ends of their lives,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 548270,
    "dDurationMs": 5890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or, presumably, when surrounded by giant alien astroengineering projects."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 554160,
    "dDurationMs": 6710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s see what a Dyson sphere would actually do to a star on the HR diagram."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 560870,
    "dDurationMs": 4450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At visible wavelengths a star’s color might not change much - it’ll just look dimmer."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 565320,
    "dDurationMs": 6260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So a star might appear too faint for its apparent temperature - dropping below the main sequence."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 571580,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But if we measure its colour using an infrared\nwavelength along with our visible light, we’d"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 578240,
    "dDurationMs": 2700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "find too much of that IR."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 580940,
    "dDurationMs": 5380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The star would look cooler - lower temperature than suggested by its visible color alone."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 586320,
    "dDurationMs": 4830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Jun Jugaku and Shiro Nishimura, Japanese astronomers who’ve been searching for Dyson spheres"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 591150,
    "dDurationMs": 6070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for years, calculate that a partial sphere\nthat intercepts only 1% of its star's light"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 597220,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "could shift the visible-to-infrared color\nby a factor of more than two."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 601260,
    "dDurationMs": 7110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Over the decades, several teams have used successively more advanced instruments to look for stars that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 608370,
    "dDurationMs": 8270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "were both too dim and too infrared, time and again they came up short. But the search has expanded massively. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 616640,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One of the latest efforts led by Swedish astronomer Erik Zackrisson looked at over 200,000 of the stars"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 624080,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the gigantic survey conducted by the Gaia\nsatellite."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 628820,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The team started by looking for stars that\nwere a little too faint for their visible-light"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 633320,
    "dDurationMs": 2760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "color - below the main sequence."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 636080,
    "dDurationMs": 3010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They found a couple of promising candidates."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 639090,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then they checked whether these stars had an excess of light in the infrared."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 644810,
    "dDurationMs": 1310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And … they did not."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 646120,
    "dDurationMs": 1820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Not enough at any rate."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 647940,
    "dDurationMs": 5570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Apparently there is non-Dyson-sphere-related\nweirdness going on, which may be interesting,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 653510,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but still isn’t aliens."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 656150,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One other approach to finding partial Dyson\nspheres is to look for strange variations"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 660470,
    "dDurationMs": 5660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in brightness and colour over time, as might\nbe expected if a giant orbiting structure"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 666130,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "partially eclipses the star in an irregular\nway."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 669650,
    "dDurationMs": 4110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that’s exactly what happened with the famed Tabby's Star."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 673760,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This weird star discovered by Tabetha Boyajian, exhibited complex dips in brightness, causing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 679820,
    "dDurationMs": 2840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rumours of alien megastructures to abound."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 682660,
    "dDurationMs": 5970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in 2019 it became somewhat clear this dimming was probably due to the debris of a tidally"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 688630,
    "dDurationMs": 2150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "disrupted moon-size body."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 690780,
    "dDurationMs": 3010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Yup, still no Dyson spheres."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 693790,
    "dDurationMs": 6550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At this point, our surveys have found no evidence for star-spanning megastructures."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 700340,
    "dDurationMs": 4870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And with new surveys that are being planned,\nwithin a decade or so we’ll either discover"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 705210,
    "dDurationMs": 5190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one or rule out anything interesting in the\nlocal part of the Milky Way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 710400,
    "dDurationMs": 3660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that doesn’t mean Dyson spheres aren’t out there."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 714060,
    "dDurationMs": 4510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It would be incredibly difficult to detect\nsuch an object around a low mass star if it’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 718570,
    "dDurationMs": 2500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on the other side of the galaxy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 721070,
    "dDurationMs": 5230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What we do know is that these structures are\nnot very common through the Milky Way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 726300,
    "dDurationMs": 2590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But that may not be true of other galaxies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 728890,
    "dDurationMs": 3669,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are hundreds of billions of galaxies\nin the observable universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 732559,
    "dDurationMs": 4272,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Unfortunately, we can't identify a single Dyson sphere's effects"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 736831,
    "dDurationMs": 4220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "around individual stars at distant galaxies. "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 741051,
    "dDurationMs": 6459,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But what if a civilization in one of those has occupied its entire home galaxy and built a huge"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 747510,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "number of Dyson spheres, or similarly energy-hungry megastructures?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 753210,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "With this possibility in mind, a team led\nby Penn State astronomer Jason Write used"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 758730,
    "dDurationMs": 5260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the WISE survey to look for galaxies that\nhad too much infrared light compared to their"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 763990,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "visible light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 764990,
    "dDurationMs": 3790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now this is a bit tougher, because galaxies aren’t as simple as stars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 768780,
    "dDurationMs": 4410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There can be a huge variation in the amount of IR that a galaxy produces."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 773190,
    "dDurationMs": 7460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But even accounting for this, the team found no evidence for galaxy-spanning civilizations"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 780650,
    "dDurationMs": 3690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in the 100,000 galaxies that they analysed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 784340,
    "dDurationMs": 3150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "No very energy-hungry ones at any rate."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 787490,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The search for galactic empires is expanding."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 791290,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the past couple of years researchers have\nbeen thinking about Dyson spheres built around"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 795290,
    "dDurationMs": 4130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the supermassive black holes in the centers\nof galaxies."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 799420,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And about the effect on the enclosed star\ndue to a partial Dyson sphere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 805020,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These are all long shots, and honestly might\nseem a bit hopelessly science-fictiony."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 811320,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But some very serious scientists - starting\nwith Freeman Dyson and Carl Sagan - take the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 816300,
    "dDurationMs": 1710,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "question very seriously."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 818010,
    "dDurationMs": 2530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For good reason too."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 820540,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The discovery of a single alien megastructure\nof any sort would massively change the way"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 825420,
    "dDurationMs": 3441,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we think about our place in the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 828861,
    "dDurationMs": 5729,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It would also point to a possible future for\nhumanity - something we might want to do when"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 834590,
    "dDurationMs": 1400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we grow up."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 835990,
    "dDurationMs": 5550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It would show us that we might even have a\nfuture - one that could even leave our own"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 841540,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "astroengineering mark on the galaxy, perhaps\nto be noticed by younger species when they"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 847540,
    "dDurationMs": 5187,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "emerge in distant, future parts of space time."
    } ]
  } ]
}
